.lua
book.chapter(7, "Tacit programming with numbered parameters")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Numbered formals remove local naming"
.pp
A tacit definition refers to its formal arguments with $1, $2, and subsequent
one-based indices. The parser discovers the highest referenced position and
elaborates an appropriate function. The resulting function participates in the
same inference, application, and module machinery as a named function. Tacit
notation changes how parameters are written; it does not disable typing or
introduce a second evaluator for arithmetic.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[add :: Int -> Int -> Int
add := $1 + $2
square := $1 * $1
named_square value := value * value
Test.equal "two parameters" (add 20 22) 42
Test.equal "equivalent styles" (square 9) (named_square 9)]=],
    "Tacit definitions with one and two parameters")
.endlua
.pp
Read the first example by substituting parameter roles, not by imagining a
stack machine. The first supplied argument occupies formal position one and
the second occupies position two. Repeated occurrences of $1 denote the same
argument value. Because ordinary application is eager, the expression that
produced that argument has already been evaluated; mentioning $1 twice does
not rerun it or consume two different input values.
.sh 2 "Arity follows the largest index"
.pp
An occurrence of $3 requires a function with at least three formal positions,
even when $1 and $2 do not occur in the body. Sparse numbering therefore
creates intentionally unused arguments. This can be convenient for adapting
a callback signature, but it can also conceal a simple mistake. An explicit
type and a small test make the intended arity clear. The implementation bounds
indices to 1024 and rejects zero; these are validation rules, not style advice.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[third :: Int -> Int -> Int -> Int
third := $3
keep := $1
Test.equal "sparse numbering" (third 10 20 30) 30
Test.equal "return argument" (keep "payload") "payload"
reorder := ($2, $1)
Test.equal "reuse as data" (reorder 7 "seven") ("seven", 7)]=],
    "Formal positions determine the generated interface")
book.algorithm("Elaborate a tacit binding", {
    "Identify numbered references belonging to this binding.",
    "Validate that every index is positive and within the implementation bound.",
    "Find the largest referenced index n.",
    "Create n formal parameter positions.",
    "Resolve each numbered occurrence to its corresponding formal.",
    "Type-check the resulting function using the ordinary function rules."
})
.endlua
.pp
Elaboration is a useful mental model even though implementation details include
scope tracking, signatures, and special handling for nested expressions. The
essential invariant is that numbered references resolve to formal arguments,
not to whatever values happened to be printed by the REPL earlier. A reusable
function must behave the same way when called from a fresh session, a module,
or a native embedding with no interactive result history.
.sh 2 "Use one parameter style per binding"
.pp
A computation that mixes a named formal with numbered references is rejected
with M0201. The implementation has some accommodation for declared parameter
names used merely as placeholders for an otherwise fully numbered body, but
that spelling communicates less clearly than a purely tacit definition.
Choose named formals or numbered formals consistently within a binding. If a
helper needs descriptive names, write it as a separate named function.
.lua
book.code("metaml-error:M0201", [=[mixed value := value + $2]=],
    "A genuinely mixed parameter style is rejected")
.endlua
.pp
This rule is easiest to remember as a scope rule rather than a formatting
preference. A reader should not have to reconstruct whether value and $1 are
aliases, whether $2 is a second declared parameter, or whether a numbered
reference accidentally belongs to a surrounding definition. Clarity at the
binding boundary is especially important when callbacks and nested closures
appear. Named lambdas are often the better choice once the body has several
independent local roles.
.sh 2 "Tacit does not mean inaccessible"
.pp
Short projections and arithmetic wrappers are natural tacit functions. Their
meaning fits in one glance and the type states what the positions represent.
Longer functions often benefit from a named interface even if they internally
call tacit helpers. A useful library can mix those styles between definitions
without imposing a project-wide preference for either extreme. The goal is
to expose the algorithm, not to maximize or minimize the number of identifiers.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[distance_squared := $1 * $1 + $2 * $2
score := $1.score
Test.equal "coordinate calculation" (distance_squared 3 4) 25
Test.equal "record projection" (score { name = "Ada", score = 42 }) 42
Test.equal "tacit callback" (List.map score [{score = 1}, {score = 2}]) [1, 2]]=],
    "Small functions whose positions remain obvious")
.endlua
.pp
When a function argument is itself callable, its numbered reference is still
a value. Applying or composing that value follows the same type-directed
rules as a named function argument. A signature can make the intended relation
explicit. Do not infer from the word concatenative that $n dynamically indexes
the evaluator's observable stack. The formal-parameter contract remains in
force in every supported context.
.sh 2 "Distinguish dollars from section holes"
.pp
The next chapter introduces #1 and #2 inside parenthesized sections. Those
holes are local to the section that elaborates them, whereas numbered dollars
refer to a tacit definition's formal positions. Both can produce ordinary
functions, but they choose their binding boundary differently. A section is
often best for a local callback such as reversing two operator operands; a
tacit definition is best when naming a reusable positional computation.
.pp
When reviewing code, first identify the binder, then resolve the placeholder.
This habit scales to compiler work: a syntax transformation that copies a
placeholder without preserving its binding context can silently change
meaning. Explicit AST representations and scope-aware passes are the tools
for avoiding such errors. Meta-programming becomes safer when the writer
understands the binding rules of the language being transformed.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect TacitRef handling and binding elaboration in both runtime files, and
compare the tacit definitions in metalib/int/mod.ml. Rewrite an affine
transformation in named and tacit form and test their equality over a finite
list of inputs. Then deliberately swap $1 and $2 and choose a test that detects
the change. An addition-only example is a poor detector because commutativity
can hide the mistake; subtraction or string concatenation exposes ordering.
